WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.770
- In early 2022,

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:04.320
action star and Hollywood
legend Bruce Willis

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.850
stepped away from acting.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.010
This piece of news was
particularly poignant

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.310
because of the reason for
his retirement: aphasia.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.360
A language disorder

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.820
that affects a person's
ability to communicate,

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.550
often caused by strokes,
brain injuries, tumors,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.500
or in Willis's case, dementia.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.120
Even though the word language comes

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.740
from the Latin for tongue,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.020
language is really more
related to the brain

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.760
than any other part of the body.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.680
It's where language happens.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.390
Whether you're shaping
words with your mouth,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.620
signing them with your hands,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.180
or writing them on paper,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.610
the bulk of the work is going on up here.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.650
While there is no one part of the brain

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.750
that handles everything speech-related,

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.720
there are some areas that are
more involved than others.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.400
Scientists have known this long before

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.570
the development of MRIs

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.630
and other brain scanning
technologies. How?

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.130
Through the study and observation

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.560
of patients who have
experienced language impairments

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.670
due to brain damage.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.250
By determining what has
been lost or impaired,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.950
neuroscientists have been
better able to understand

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.750
how our brains process language

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.340
and what language really is.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.200
And now my brain-language connection

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.090
will communicate that to you.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.410
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:08.836
and this is "Otherwords."

00:01:08.836 --> 00:01:11.419
(upbeat music)

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.030
To learn more about the relationship

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.710
between language and the brain,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.900
I spoke with Dr. Alexander Tan,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.640
a pediatric neuropsychologist

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.530
with expertise in the assessment

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.990
of neurological and
neurodevelopmental disorders

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.730
in children and adolescents.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:33.930
So you're a neuropsychologist.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.030
What is neuropsychology?

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.550
- The definition of
neuropsychology literally is

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:42.180
the study of brain and
behavior and that relationship.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.640
So as a pediatric neuropsychologist,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.920
we do checkups on
different brain functions,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.550
like attention, memory,
visual perception, language.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.320
And then using those results,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.780
we can assess for brain disorders

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.970
and make sure that we are
getting children the treatments

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.230
and supports that they might need

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.020
and track their development
or their recovery over time.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.400
- When it comes to the connection

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.050
between language and the brain,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.500
the first answers were discovered
in sort of a curious way.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.900
Prior to technological
advances in neuroimaging,

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.670
like functional MRIs,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.130
doctors couldn't see what
was happening in the brain

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.040
and observe what areas lit
up with various stimuli.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.280
The only way to look at the
brain was by opening it up.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.510
In the 19th century,

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.030
physicians Paul Broca and Carl Wernicke

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.340
separately discovered
two regions of the brain

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.100
through conducting autopsies
on aphasic patients.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.650
- [Dr. Tan] Dr. Broca and
Dr. Wernicke were able

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.690
to locate specific parts of the brain

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.320
that have resulting
impacts on their ability to

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.920
either express language
or to comprehend language.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.710
And those parts, now
referred to as Broca's area

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.530
and Wernicke's area,
are in the left frontal

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.170
or left temporal lobe, respectively.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.960
And that was one of
the first signals to us

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.770
that the left hemisphere
is dominant for language.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.080
- For more than 95% of right-handed people

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.270
and 60% of left-handed people,

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.180
the left hemisphere is
dominant for language.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.170
But that's not to say

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.900
that the language party is
exclusively on the left side.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.930
- Now that we've had more
advancements in technology,

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.700
different kinds of imaging techniques,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.420
we now are seeing that language
actually involves a lot

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.620
of different parts of the brain,

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.680
including, you know,
implicating all four lobes,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.270
the gray matter and the white matter,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.520
cortical and subcortical structures.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.310
It's very much a complex ability

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.530
that involves communication
and coordination

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.930
between a lot of different
parts of the brain.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.120
- When you see an image, say, this one,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.250
you think, "Okay, yeah, sure, it's a ball,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.110
or a car, or a unicorn."

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.640
But that's just the result.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.520
Let's backtrack to what
happened on the inside.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.800
First, you process the visual input

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.720
so that you could recognize the object.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.350
Then you have to convert the
input into a semantic concept.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.840
From there, you search
through your mental Rolodex

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.670
of abstract representations

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.010
and pick the concept matching word.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.290
Finally, you articulate the phonemes

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.000
that make up that word.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.370
And if just one step
doesn't work properly,

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.470
you might not be able
to recognize the object,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:05.970
or know what it's for,

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.380
or find the word you need,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.600
or communicate the word to anyone else.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.380
What is aphasia, first of
all, and what causes it?

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.130
- So aphasia is essentially

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.200
an impairment in language abilities.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.220
And it is typically caused by strokes,

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.980
brain injuries that affect
certain parts of the brain,

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.610
or some kind of
neurodegenerative condition,

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.160
like dementias or Alzheimer's disease.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.600
- [Dr. Brozovsky] The two
most common types of aphasias

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.870
are the ones named after
Broca and Wernicke.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.480
Damage to Broca's area
may cause Broca's aphasia,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.190
or expressive aphasia,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.710
which means you can
generally understand others

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.990
but have difficulty
getting your words out.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.900
It might be limited to single
words or short phrases.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.850
On the other hand, someone suffering

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.020
from Wernicke's aphasia,

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.520
or receptive aphasia,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.670
will have trouble understanding
language, even their own,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.850
but is still capable of
fluid, competent speech.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.830
They may talk in long, nonsensical,

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.170
yet grammatically correct sentences.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.460
- [Dr. Tan] There's a white matter highway

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.500
between Broca's area and
Wernicke's area called

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.833
the arcuate fasciculus
that can be communicates

00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:11.250
between the two.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.860
And so sometimes when
there's impact to that,

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.600
there's something called
conduction aphasia

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.300
where our expression is intact,

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.770
our comprehension is intact,

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.420
but because we can't integrate the two,

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.840
then it's hard for us to
repeat language that we hear.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:28.410
- In other cases, extensive
deterioration of the brain,

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.240
such as various types of dementia,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.770
can lead to global aphasia

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.210
where all three factors,

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.090
fluency, understanding, and
repetition are impaired.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:37.680
And what kind of treatments are available

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.350
for people who are experiencing aphasia?

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.780
- Our main treatment for
the aphasia specifically

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.180
usually focuses on rehabilitation

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.310
in the form of speech
and language therapy.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.640
We always want to have
supportive treatments in place

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.620
for some of the side effects.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.450
Individuals with aphasia

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:58.590
or at risk for things like
depression and social isolation,

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.330
you know, our brains deteriorate faster

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.373
with the absence of social connection,

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:07.530
so it's really important
for individuals with aphasia

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.390
and for those supporting them to find,

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.940
you know, alternate ways
to communicate with them

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.890
and continue to integrate them socially.

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:17.010
And just making sure that
we're patient with them

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.900
as they try to find the words.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.430
- We know that the brain changes

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.050
over the course of our lives

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.790
and it generally comes to maturity

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:25.590
in the mid to late twenties.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.110
During childhood, our neuroplasticity,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.910
or the ability of networks in the brain

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.350
to grow and change,

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.420
is at its highest levels.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.360
- If, for example, children
have seizures on the left side,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:37.620
there's this crowding effect

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:38.760
that we sometimes observe

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.830
where language kind of reorganizes

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.410
onto the right side of the
brain to preserve itself

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.410
and sometimes crowds
out the other functions

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.750
that were typically associated
with the right side.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:50.730
So sometimes resulting in weaknesses

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.740
to non-verbal and visual skills.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.960
- So is the brain prioritizing language

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.300
above significant other
functions in general?

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.810
Is that something that the
brain generally prioritizes?

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.190
- Yes, the brain does tend

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:06.062
to try to preserve language abilities.

00:07:06.062 --> 00:07:07.260
You know, language is so important

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.120
to be able to communicate,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.550
to learn, to navigate the world socially.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.310
The brain will reorganize however it can

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.590
to make sure language
function is preserved.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.350
- Is it possible to improve
your neuroplasticity

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.280
as an adult?

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.517
- Yeah, sure, there's always ways that,

00:07:21.517 --> 00:07:24.060
you know, the typical things
that our doctors tell us

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.470
that we never listen to, right?

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.850
Making sure that we're really
taking care of our sleep,

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:31.830
our exercise, our diet.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.200
Learning a new language
is a really great one

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.060
that really forces our brain

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.850
to activate all those parts of the brain

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.490
that are really important for
not just language learning

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.760
but also code switching
and executive functioning.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:48.360
- The brain is an incredibly
capable and resilient machine,

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.190
and scientists are learning new things

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.620
about the brain-language
connection every day.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.570
What is something you've
learned about the human brain

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:55.620
over the course of your career

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.540
that you think everyone should know?

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.600
- The number one thing I've
learned about the brain

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.570
is just that there's always
more to learn about the brain.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.420
We've made big, you
know, very large strides

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.420
in terms of understanding how
parts of the brain correlate

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:10.590
with brain function,

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.560
but there are just so many
answers that we still don't have,

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.410
and that's just what makes it
eternally fascinating to me.

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.380
- Language is one of the
things that makes us human,

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:20.370
and because of that,

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.130
it is invaluable to us as a species.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.820
Any loss of language or
communication ability is a tragedy.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.460
But by the groundwork laid
through studying language

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.080
through the lens of brain damage,

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.850
we've come to understand the pieces

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:34.530
that make up human language,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.060
how injury affects them,

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.800
and how to apply that information

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.203
to improve the lives of
those who are affected.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.920
You may also remember
from a previous episode

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.170
that swearing relies just as much

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.640
on the right side of the brain.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.800
So don't expect someone
with left-side brain image

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.240
to swear any left.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:53.243
Left? Oh no! (laughs)
(crew laughs)

00:08:53.243 --> 00:08:55.826
(bright music)

